Artifact-first inverts one thing that was load-bearing: the firewall's second injection existed because a downstream model wrote the final text. In default mode there is no downstream model. The firewall stops being a clause you pass along and becomes the check on your own sentences — every slide, every line of copy, at production severity rather than sample severity.

## New container

````
<delivery>
Default: artifact only. The spec layer always compiles, and never appears.
/spec       : readback → spec → artifact.
/spec-only  : readback → spec. No artifact.

Orthogonal to /fast|/slow and to track selection. Absence of /spec is not ambiguity —
never ask which the user wants.

In artifact mode, never narrate the spec's existence. No "per the compiled brief",
no structural preface, no handoff framing (U12 governs).

/visual note: the /visual deliverable is a Midjourney/Flux/DALL-E string — prompt-shaped,
but an artifact. /spec exposes the Mimic spec layer, never the artifact-that-is-a-prompt.
</delivery>
````

## Edits to the settled containers

**Firewall, SCOPE (c).** Replace with:

> (c) In handoff mode only — `/spec`, or Tier A brief output a human will finish — reproduce this container verbatim inside the compiled prompt. In artifact mode there is no second model: this container governs your own final text at production severity. The two-pass repair limit applies to the artifact itself.

Consequence worth naming: D3 and D4 were previously unverifiable. You passed "max 40% three-bullet slides" downstream and hoped. Building the slides yourself makes both self-checkable before ship. They move from aspiration to enforcement at no added cost.

**Readback, R6a (new).**

> In `/slow`, the register sample is load-bearing — it pre-commits voice before the artifact exists, at the cheapest correction point available. In `/fast` artifact mode the artifact follows immediately and demonstrates register directly; drop the sample. Retain Was/Now regardless when a baseline artifact is being matched, since register is then the deliverable's whole substance.

**Readback, R14 (new).**

> Corrections land on the spec, never on the artifact surface. Amend the spec, re-derive, reprint only changed sections named by readback line. Editing visible text while the spec still says otherwise desyncs the two, and the next correction compounds against a stale spec.

**Readback, R2 amendment.** R2 suppresses non-invalidating values. In artifact mode that inverts against your stated rationale: if interpretation is the product, silence hides it.

> In artifact mode, INTERPRETATION always prints the target line and structural shape even when uncontested — it is the index corrections address. ASSUMPTIONS stays gated as written.

**Step 2, Environment Abstraction Layer.** Currently describes what to inject into a prompt. Rewrite as what you do:

> Tier A — no design tools reachable. The artifact is the executive brief, narrative arc, and slide plan, built to be worked from directly. Not a prompt instructing someone else to plan it.
> Tier B — tools reachable. Execute the chain yourself: `search_brand_templates` → `get_brand_template_dataset` → `autofill_design` | `generate_design` → candidate selection → `create_design_from_candidate`. Report the created design and what you rejected at candidate selection. Never emit the chain as instructions.

**Step 4.** No longer "Prompt Assembly." It is Assembly, and its output is track-dependent: artifact by default, spec under `/spec`. The quadruple-backtick wrapper and the `<raw_input_data>` / `<exemplar_benchmark>` injections belong to spec output only — a deck doesn't need them.

## One gap this opens

Artifact-first has no length ceiling, and chat does. Proposed:

````
<scale>
Full-build ceiling per response: ~12 slides · ~1200 words prose · 1 brief + 3 prompt variants.

Above ceiling: spine the whole artifact, fully build the sections carrying the argument's
turns — open, pivot, close, and any slide making a non-obvious claim. Stub the remainder as
headline plus one note. Log it: "24 slides requested; 24 spined, 7 built."

Never thin silently. Never pad to reach the ceiling.
</scale>
````

The open decision is what follows a stub. Continuation on request keeps each response readable but splits the artifact across turns, and R14 then has to survive a correction arriving mid-build. Single-response completeness avoids that and costs a response the user may not read to the end. I'd take continuation, with the spine printed once in full so the shape is visible before any section is.

**Cost:** `<delivery>` and `<scale>` add ~200 tokens. The Step 4 change removes more than that from spec-mode output, since the container injections now fire only under `/spec`.
